WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:10.840
So hi! Good morning everybody
and welcome back.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:16.209
So I'm Lee Woodley I work at AAAS
and I'm really interested in both the

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:20.200
technical so I think online tools and
the human so I think people community

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.730
managers who really make scientific
collaborations, scientific teamwork,

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:30.939
scientific communities actually function
productively. So I've been invited to

00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:35.350
talk here this morning about communities
of practice and as Jeff has outlined

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:39.100
that's really because what we want to do
now for the remainder of the conference

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:44.469
is think about what comes next.
You know we've had these productive,

00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:51.730
useful, generative conversations but what
now and the notion of a community of

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:55.449
practice is something that has been
kicked around for at least a couple of

00:00:55.449 --> 00:00:59.140
years but we've never really had
explicit conversations about what does

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:03.370
that mean? What does that look like? How
might we actually create that and

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:07.960
participate in it? So what I'm going to
do is I'm essentially going to give you

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:13.420
a ten-minute primer on communities of
practice theory. So the notion here isn't

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:18.130
to be top-down. It's not about saying you know there's a right way and a way that we

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:21.100
can do this.
It's to give us a framework. It's to get

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.240
us back to the theory so that we're
thinking about this from the same

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.080
perspective and we can use that then as
a way of opening up the dialogue about

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:33.460
what things we might want to use and
where we may decide to go forward from

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.820
here and listening to this and going
actually we're not community perhaps at

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:41.860
all is a completely valid response. So
there's no assumptions about the the

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:46.630
output here. So what I'm going to be doing is drawing on the communities of practice

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:50.500
literature. Primarily this is the work of
Etienne Wenger and colleagues so they've

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:55.390
been thinking a lot about knowledge
generation and social learning over the

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:59.260
years and have come up with a lot of
the foundational ideas about communities

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:04.600
of practice and this talk is going to
break out into three key takeaways and

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:08.890
those takeaways are designed to match
along with the core themes of the

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:14.440
conference. So we have models, methods, and measures. So I'm gonna start first of all

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:18.730
talking about methods. So that's the why
why. Why do we need communities of

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:23.230
practice ever? How do we deal with knowledge generation?

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:28.629
How do we deal with the cultivation and
the the use of knowledge? Then I'm going

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:32.409
to move on and I'm going to outline the
model for communities of practice. So

00:02:32.409 --> 00:02:36.549
communities of practice have a strict
definition. We think about them in a

00:02:36.549 --> 00:02:40.870
particular way so I'm gonna elucidate
that model and drop in a few questions

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:44.889
that might help us start thinking about
what that might apply to to us in this

00:02:44.889 --> 00:02:51.430
science outreach space and then finally
the final thing is measures. So really

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:55.599
getting to evaluation and just touching
on this idea of well okay if we do go

00:02:55.599 --> 00:02:59.200
down this route and we do decide that we
want to do something more together, how

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.510
are we gonna know whether it's working
or not okay? So let's start at the

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:08.889
beginning, so let's start with the
methods and thinking about knowledge

00:03:08.889 --> 00:03:13.959
management. So if you think about how
generative hopefully the the past couple

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:17.469
of days have been for you and you think
about the the conversations that we've

00:03:17.469 --> 00:03:21.370
had as a result of the lightning talks,
around the tables, in the corridors, at

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:25.989
Story Collider, out on the terrace, over
lunch and you think about what you gain

00:03:25.989 --> 00:03:30.519
from that, really we've been iterating
knowledge together. We've been dropping

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:34.750
in our own experiences the things that
we've done, sharing those with others, and

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:39.099
and building on that together and that
really fits in with the five ways in

00:03:39.099 --> 00:03:45.040
which we think about knowledge. So first
of all knowledge can't be codified

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.299
simply as a static object. So if we'd
given you all a bit of paper or a laptop

00:03:49.299 --> 00:03:52.870
and we said write down everything you know about science outreach that wouldn't

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:56.829
really be very useful and if we'd put
that all together in one place that

00:03:56.829 --> 00:04:01.359
isn't gonna make any one of us any
better at doing science outreach. So we

00:04:01.359 --> 00:04:04.780
think about knowledge as not being able
to be broken down into that single

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:10.000
static thing. Part of the reason for that
is that knowledge as I'm sure you're

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.530
well aware can be both explicit so the
kind of things that we choose to codify

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:20.470
in publications, in posters, in
presentations and implicit so the kind

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:23.020
of things that we learn through
interacting with each other, through

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:26.969
having conversations, through talking
about how things are done around here.

00:04:26.969 --> 00:04:31.910
As a result knowledge is therefore also
iterative, so like the process

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:36.320
of science itself you know we we may
start with a core set of assumptions,

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.110
core set of things that we know but
we're always building on those. We're

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:43.550
always testing them, adding on a bit from
somebody else, trying that out, coming up

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:48.260
with new knowledge and that means that
knowledge the generation of knowledge is

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:53.840
a social endeavor. You know we don't sit
in our dreaded silos and learn stuff by

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:58.760
ourselves. We learn it through
interacting with each other and then

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:03.350
finally for knowledge to be maximally
useful we talk about how important it is

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:08.570
for knowledge to get out of those silos
and actually cross boundaries into other

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:13.010
areas, so knowledge is maximally useful
when we can reuse it and apply it to

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:16.940
different situations and so the reason
that I'm outlining all of this is that

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:20.750
communities of practice, this notion of
them, it has arisen as a way of

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:26.240
cultivating and managing knowledge
together. So a community of practice comes

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.740
together around doing and managing
knowledge in these ways that I've just

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:36.740
described. So to end the first section,
some questions. So do we think we need a

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:42.260
community of practice in terms of
bubbling up the various expertise that

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:45.620
we have in this room and beyond the room
for the folks that weren't able to be

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:49.280
here? Does a community of practice sound
like a framework that might be helpful?

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.230
And then reflecting on your own needs
and your own expertise, you know, what

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:58.220
knowledge do you have to give and in in
what format? Gow have you most enjoyed

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:01.250
sharing things here? You know maybe you
ended up in conversations about things

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:03.740
that you didn't realize you were going
to talk about and these are things that

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:07.580
you want to take forward and then what
would you like to get in terms of

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.850
gaining knowledge from other people? Do
you know what you don't know and do you

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:16.850
know how you might go about finding it
out? Okay so the second section as I say

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:20.090
we're going to talk about models. So I
specifically want to outline what we

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:25.130
mean when we talk about a community of
practice and so venue and colleagues

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:30.440
describe a community of practice as
having three key structural elements. So

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:36.080
they talked about the notion of a domain,
a community, and a practice. So the domain:

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:41.260
this is really what it is we are focused
on. So for a community of practice to be

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:45.779
most effective we need to know
why is that we're gathering. This really

00:06:45.779 --> 00:06:51.479
gets at the the purpose, the goals of why
we're coming together as a community and

00:06:51.479 --> 00:06:56.430
Mallon gives us an identifier identity.
So we should self-identify as being part

00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:00.539
of a community of practice and that
allows us to know why it is that we are

00:07:00.539 --> 00:07:05.849
here. That also allows people looking in
from the outside so other stakeholders

00:07:05.849 --> 00:07:07.529
who may be interested in what we're
doing

00:07:07.529 --> 00:07:12.089
to see that there's legitimacy in that
so we we've created a definition of why

00:07:12.089 --> 00:07:15.419
we're doing what we're doing. So one
of the questions that I would ask about

00:07:15.419 --> 00:07:21.029
this is is science outreach our domain
or is that too big? Is that is that too

00:07:21.029 --> 00:07:24.080
amorphous? Is there too much going on
there? Are we actually talking about

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.949
multiple sub communities of practice
focused on different areas, different

00:07:28.949 --> 00:07:32.969
types of outreach, different audiences
for outreach? I don't want to presuppose

00:07:32.969 --> 00:07:38.699
what that looks like but if we use this
framework to think about this how might

00:07:38.699 --> 00:07:43.830
that influence what we we might want to
create after this meeting. So a second

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:47.430
structural element of a community of
practice is the community. So we've

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:51.509
talked a lot over the last couple of
days about reaching out to underserved

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:55.709
audiences, making sure that we're
including all voices in the room, making

00:07:55.709 --> 00:07:59.729
sure that we don't make assumptions but
that we listen deeply and therefore we

00:07:59.729 --> 00:08:04.559
learn from from everybody that we bring
together. So once we've identified a

00:08:04.559 --> 00:08:08.789
particular domain or various subdomains
then we need to be quite intentional

00:08:08.789 --> 00:08:13.229
about saying well who do we need there
around that table to help us advance our

00:08:13.229 --> 00:08:18.779
knowledge to cultivate our activities in
that particular domain. And then the

00:08:18.779 --> 00:08:23.729
final piece as I hope is following on
quite logically is the practice. So

00:08:23.729 --> 00:08:26.789
that's okay we know what domain is. We
know who we've got around the table. What

00:08:26.789 --> 00:08:31.860
are we actually gonna do together? Just
standing at an online site and putting a

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:35.130
bunch of documents in there that's not a
practice. You know we talked about

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:38.039
cultivation of knowledge through the
ways I described at the beginning,

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:43.169
through interactions, through building
something, through trying to tackle a

00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:47.550
particular problem. You know do we want
to work on it so we working on

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:50.639
evaluation models and comparing what
we've each done in our different

00:08:50.639 --> 00:08:55.500
projects.
Do we want perhaps to you know think

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.840
about writing something together? You
know then

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.280
needs to be an activity or a set of
activities which may include meetings,

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:06.620
may include annual conferences, may
include webinars, may include live chats

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:10.590
but we need to be regularly doing
something together to cultivate this

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:16.020
core knowledge base that we have. Okay
so now is section 2 so that's thinking

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:20.250
about three structural elements of what
a COP is and then I promise the final

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:24.180
thing that I was going to end with was
touching on the the measures theme or

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:29.070
thinking about the evaluation. And so one
of the signs of a healthy community as

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:32.310
it gets set up and as it gets to
maturity, is that we were able to be

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:34.920
self-reflective.
We're able to kind of say well is this

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:39.030
working for me? Why am I here? Are we are
we really doing things that are useful?

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:46.200
And with a community of practice then
firstly obviously we need to identify

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:51.150
the shared goals for a community of
practice but it's not just about saying

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:55.560
well are we producing the output, the the
report, the conversations, the thing that

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:59.580
we said we were going to do. So it's not
just about saying you know let's let's

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:03.720
count clicks, let's count comments, let's
count you know what's going on there

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:08.700
because we think about knowledge as I
said as spreading out beyond silos. So if

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:12.630
we come together like at a conference
like this and we share knowledge, what

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:16.260
happens to that knowledge afterwards? So
what are you going to do with what you

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:21.060
learned here? How is that knowledge that
that is being created within a potential

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:24.870
community of practice helping you get
better at your day job or helping you

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:29.580
tell somebody at your Institute how they
could go and look something up or talk

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:33.000
to this other person and learn this
thing? We call now the concept of a

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.150
double-knit knowledge organization so
that you're in the community of practice

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:40.110
and we're cultivating knowledge together
but then we take it out beyond the

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:43.260
community of practice and we weave it
into what we actually do in the outside

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:47.340
world. And that creates really
interesting self-reflection and

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:51.420
evaluation questions because we don't
just measure what goes on in the

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:54.000
community of practice in the group, but
we start thinking about what that looks

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.370
like out in the world. Okay so that's the
end of the primer that was communities of

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:04.890
practice 101. So what I encourage us to
do and Ben's gonna help us think about

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:07.450
it's like ground this now in practical
reality and

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:11.950
what this actually looks like but just
think about you know as a result of the

00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:16.420
conversations that you've had here, what
might this communities of practice

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:21.250
notion mean to you. Were we thinking
about sub projects? Is something has

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:25.110
something occurred to you you really
love to work on and that you'd love to

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:30.720
cultivate together with other people? So
thank you for listening.

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:52.020
I was just wondering um like how do we
build a community without making it

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:58.000
without make putting up extra barriers.
Like when people see oh it's already a

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:03.670
community like I can't join type of thinking? I think there's a there's definitely a

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:08.560
balance to be struck here so we need to
create a strong enough sense of identity

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:11.560
that we I guess a we self-identify as
being part of something, we feel

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:14.860
committed to it, we want to keep coming
back, we want to work together but we

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:19.090
also need to think about you know having
a certain humility that maybe as we

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:21.850
